          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Problem framing and scale selection",
          "content": "A research team proposes a lighter structural alloy from calculations with unknown boundary conditions. Determine what performance must be reproduced, which length scales control failure, and how discretization, numerical error, input uncertainty and experiments will be tracked before screening candidates. No materials simulation is interpretable until its target observable, reference material state, environmental range and intended downstream decision are fixed. Distinguish a calculated ideal crystal from an actual manufactured material, a numerical fit from independent validation, and a candidate ranking from an authorized design decision. A model should be evaluated by whether it rules out alternatives or informs an observable experiment, not by the complexity of its software or resolution of its graphics.\n\nThe unit's evidence record starts with **model specification, nondimensional units and boundary checks**. Collect **input state, governing laws, mesh/basis/timestep convergence, solver tolerance and validation observables** and prioritize tests such as **analytic limits, matched independent experiments and conservation residuals**. The most dangerous untested assumption is **whether stable numerical output is actually a prediction of the relevant physical material state**. Work backward from that possible failure: identify at least two competing explanations, a calculation that could distinguish them, an independently measurable outcome and a decision threshold. Be explicit if available observations cannot constrain the model. The five lessons progressively build a defensible case record.",
